\subsection{Main functions and their arguments}
% %
The two main functions for fitting the SPAR algorithm are:
%
\begin{itemize}
\item \fct{spar} fits the SPAR algorithm without
cross-validation and returns an \class{spar} object.
\item \fct{spar.cv} implements the cross-validated procedure and returns a `\code{spar.cv}' object.
\end{itemize}
%
Additionally, the aliases \fct{spareg} and \fct{spareg.cv} are available for convenience.
Both functions accept the following key arguments: \\
\begin{itemize}
    \item \code{x} -- an \( n \times p \) numeric matrix of predictors and
    \code{y} -- a numeric vector of length \( n \).
%
    \item \textbf{\code{family}}: An object from \fct{stats::family}; defaults to \fct{gaussian}.

    \item \textbf{\code{model}}: An object of class \class{sparmodel} specifying the model for each element of the ensemble. Defaults to \code{spar\_glm()} for Gaussian-identity and to \code{spar\_glmnet()} for others.

    \item \textbf{\code{rp}}: An object of class \class{randomprojection}. Defaults to \code{rp\_cw(data = TRUE)}.

    \item \textbf{\code{screencoef}}: An object of class \class{screencoef}. Defaults to \code{NULL} (no screening).

    \item \textbf{\code{nnu}}: Number of threshold values \( \nu \) to consider. Defaults to 20.

    \item \textbf{\code{nus}}: An optional vector of threshold values \( \nu \). If not provided, defaults to a grid of \code{nnu} values: zero and \code{nnu - 1} equally spaced quantiles of the absolute values of the non-zero marginal coefficients.

    \item \textbf{\code{nummods}}: Number of models in the ensemble. Defaults to 20. Also allows for a vector of ensemble sizes.

    \item \textbf{\code{measure}}: Performance measure for selecting \( \nu \) and \( M \). Options: \code{"deviance"} (default), \code{"mse"} (mean squared error), \code{"mae"} (mean absolute error), \code{"class"} (misclassification error), or \code{"1-auc"} (one minus area under the ROC curve).

    \item \textbf{\code{avg\_type}}: Stage of aggregation for computing the performance measure---either on \code{"link"} or \code{"response"} level.

    \item \textbf{\code{parallel}}: Logical: use parallel backend if available. Defaults to \code{FALSE}.
\end{itemize}
%
Furthermore, \code{spar()} has the specific arguments \code{xval} and  \code{yval} -- validation data for choosing $\nu_\text{best}$ and $M_\text{best}$ (if not provided, \code{x} and
\code{y} will be employed).
%

Function~\code{spar.cv()} has specific arguments
\code{nfolds}, the
number of folds to be used (defaults to 10) and
\code{fast\_fit = c("fix_rpm_and_inds", "fix_rpm", "none")},
indicating whether to use the same random projection matrix and/or
the same indices for screening across folds
(defaults to \code{"fix_rpm_and_inds"}).
%
If one of the first two options is selected,  \code{spar.cv()} uses a
lightweight version of \code{spar()} in each fold to reduce computational
overhead. Random projections and/or screening indicators are computed once on
the full dataset before cross-validation and remain fixed throughout. This is
achieved by an initial call to \code{spar()} on the entire dataset.
If data-driven projections are used, the training data for each fold will be
used to update the corresponding entries of the projection matrix.
If \code{fast_fit = "none"} is selected, the random projection and screening
indicators are recomputed in each fold.
A brief simulation study in Appendix~\ref{app:cv-fast-fit} shows that the
differences in terms of predictive performance and runtimes
among the \code{fast_fit} options are in general not large for the investigated
settings. However, if
users want to avoid target leakage \code{fast_fit = "none"} should be selected.
%

\subsection{Screening coefficients}\label{sec:soft:screen}
%
Screening coefficients in \pkg{spareg} are implemented as \proglang{S}3 classes \class{screencoef}.
These objects are created using functions like \code{screen_*()} which accept as arguments:
\code{...} (additional arguments saved as attributes of the object) and
\code{control} (a list of parameters for computing the screening coefficients).

%
The following screening coefficients are implemented in \pkg{spareg}:
%
\begin{itemize}
\item \code{screen_marglik()} -- computes the screening coefficients by the
coefficient of $\boldsymbol X_{j}$ for $j =1,\dots,p$  in a univariate GLM using the
\fct{stats::glm} function.
It allows to pass a list of controls through the \code{control} argument to
\fct{stats::glm}
e.g.,~\code{screen_marglik(control = list(family = binomial(probit)))}.
%
\item  \code{screen_cor()} -- computes the screening coefficients by the
correlation between $\boldsymbol y$ and $\boldsymbol X_{j}$ using the function \fct{stats::cor}. It
allows to pass a list of controls through the \code{control} argument to
\fct{stats::cor} -- e.g.,~\code{screen_cor(control = list(method = "spearman"))}.
%
\item  \code{screen_glmnet()} -- computes by default a ridge coefficient with a small penalty:
$$
\hat\omega=: \text{argmin}_{\beta\in \mathbb{R}^p}\text{min}_{{\beta_0}
\in\mathbb{R}}\sum_{i=1}^n -\ell(\beta;y_i,\boldsymbol x_i) + \frac{\varepsilon}{2}
\sum_{j=1}^p{\beta}_j^2, \, \varepsilon > 0
$$
This function uses \code{glmnet()} of package
\pkg{glmnet} \citep{pkg:glmnet, pkg:glmnet_glm} with
elastic net mixing parameter $\alpha =0$ (ridge penalty),
a small \code{lambda.min.ratio} (smallest penalty as a fraction of the maximum value in the grid) and
penalty selection based on \cite{parzer2024glms}: the smallest penalty where the deviance ratio is
$< 0.99$ (Gaussian) or $< 0.8$ (other families).
Custom controls can be passed  via the \code{control} argument
e.g.,~\code{screen_glmnet(control = list(alpha = 0.5))}.
\end{itemize}
%
The following attributes can be passed to the \code{screen_*()} function through \code{...}:
%
\begin{itemize}
\item \code{nscreen} number of variables to be retained
after screening; defaults to $2n$. If
$2n > p$, no screening is performed.
%
\item \code{split_data_prop},
proportion of the data to be used for computing the screening
coefficient. The remaining data will be used for estimating the marginal
models; default is to use
the whole data for
screening coefficient and the marginal model estimation.
%
\item \code{type} character -- either \code{"prob"} (for
probabilistic screening -- default)  or \code{"fixed"} (for using a fixed set of
\code{nscreen} variables).
%
\item \code{reuse_in_rp} logical -- indicates whether the screening
coefficient should be reused at a later stage in the construction of a data-dependent random
projection. Defaults to \code{FALSE}. This attribute is ignored if a data-agnostic random projection is used.
\end{itemize}
%
%
All implemented \code{screen_*()} functions return an object of class
\class{screencoef}
which in turn is a list with three elements containing a \code{generate_fun}
function, the \code{control} list passed by the user in
\code{screen_*()}, and optional metadata like \code{name}. A user-friendly \fct{print} method of the \class{screencoef} object is also provided.
% %
% <<>>=
% screen_marglik()
% @
% \begin{itemize}
% \item an optional character \code{name} used for printing;
% %
% \item \code{generate_fun()} -- function to generate a vector of screening coefficients of length $p$. Takes \code{x} (standardized predictors), \code{y}  (standardized responses), and \code{object} (the \class{screencoef} object itself).
% %
% \item  \code{control}, which is the control list passed by the user in
% \code{screen_*()}. These controls are arguments to be used in
% \code{generate_fun()}.
% \end{itemize}
%
% The structure of the object is the following:
% <<>>=
% str(obj)
% @
% Function \code{generate_fun()} defines the generation of the screening
% coefficient. Note that it considers the controls in \code{object$control} when
% calling the \code{stats::glm()} function (unless it is provided, the
% \code{family} argument in \code{stats::glm()} will be set to the ``global''
% family of the SPAR algorithm which is assigned
% inside the \code{spar()} function as an attribute for the \class{screencoef} object).
For convenience, a constructor function \code{constructor\_screencoef()}
is provided, which can be used to create new \code{screen_*()} functions.
An example is presented in Section~\ref{sec-extensscrcoef}.
%
%
\subsection{Random projections}\label{sec:soft:rps}
%
Random projection objects are
implemented as \proglang{S}3
classes \class{randomprojection} and are created by functions like \code{rp_*()}.
These functions take \code{...} and a list of controls \code{control} as arguments.
%
% \begin{Code}
% rp_*(..., control = list())
% \end{Code}
%
The package includes the following random projections:
\begin{itemize}
\item \code{rp_gaussian()} -- entries are iid from the normal distribution (defaults to standard normal).
%
\item \code{rp_sparse()} -- sparse matrix as in \cite{ACHLIOPTAS2003JL};
$\psi$ can be specified
via \code{control} e.g.,~\code{rp_sparse(control = list(psi = 1/3))}.  Defaults to  \code{psi = 1}.
%
\item \code{rp_cw()} -- sparse embedding random projection in
\cite{Clarkson2013LowRankApprox}.
If the random projection is specified as \code{rp_cw(data = TRUE)} and the
screening coefficient object has the attribute \code{reuse_in_rp = TRUE}, the
diagonal entries of $D$ in Equation~\ref{eq:rp_cw}
are set to the screening coefficients.
Otherwise, if \code{rp_cw(data = TRUE)}, the data-driven modification to the CW matrix proposed in \cite{parzer2024glms} is used.
\end{itemize}
%
Common attributes which can be passed via \code{...} to the \code{rp_*()} functions include:
%
\begin{itemize}
\item \code{mslow}: minimum dimension for projecting the predictors; defaults to
$\log(p)$. Note that for JL-type random projections, \code{mslow} can be chosen
from JL bounds.
%
\item \code{msup}: maximum dimension for projecting the predictors; defaults to
$n/2$ or. if $p < n/2$, to $p$.
%
\end{itemize}
%
% Note that for JL-type random projections, \code{mslow} can be chosen from JL bounds
% which preserve the distances between
% all pairs of points within a factor $(1 \pm \epsilon)$ (e.g.,~\cite{ACHLIOPTAS2003JL} show that for a given $\epsilon\in(0,1)$, the JL lemma is satisfied for
% $m_0 = \log n(4 + 2\tau)/(\epsilon^2/2 - \epsilon^3/3)$ with probability
% $1 - n^{-\tau}$).
%
The \code{rp_*()} functions return an object of class \class{randomprojection}
which is a list of five elements. The most important elements contain
\code{generate_fun()}, the function for generating the random projection
matrix, and \code{control}, the list of controls passed by the user in
\code{rp_*()}.  Optional elements include \code{name},
function \code{update_fun()} for updating the
\class{randomprojection} objects with further relevant information
(such as \class{family} or precomputed screening coefficients), or function
\code{update_rpm_w_data()}, which specifies how to update the pre-computed
matrices (when \code{fast_fit} is \code{fix_rpm_and_inds} or \code{fix_rpm})  with data
information in each fold of the cross-validation procedure.
% \begin{itemize}
% \item a optional character \code{name}
% for printing;
% %
% \item \code{generate_fun()} function for generating the random projection matrix . This
% function should have arguments
% \begin{itemize}
% \item \code{rp}, which is itself a \class{randomprojection}
% object;
% \item \code{m}, the target dimension;
% \item a vector of indices \code{included_vector}
% which indicates the column index of the original variables in the \code{x}
% matrix to be projected using the random projection. This is needed due to the
% fact that screening can be employed pre-projection.
% \item \code{x} and \code{y}, the matrix of standardized
% predictors and vector of  (standardized)
% responses -- can be \code{NULL} if the random projection to be generated is data-agnostic.
% \end{itemize}
% It returns a matrix or
% a sparse matrix of class \class{dgCMatrix} of the \pkg{Matrix} package
% \citep{pkg:Matrix} with \code{m} rows and \code{length(included_vector)} columns.
% %
% \item \code{control}, which is the control list in \code{rp_*()}. These controls
% are arguments needed in \code{generate_fun()} in order to generate the desired
% random projection.
% \end{itemize}
% For the case where the random projection should incorporate some information
% related to the data, two more elements can be potentially relevant.
% \begin{itemize}
% \item Function \code{update_fun()} updates the \class{randomprojection}
% object with relevant information from the arguments of the \code{spar()} or \code{spar.cv()}
% function call. If it is not provided, it defaults to updating the
% \class{randomprojection} object with a further attribute
% \code{family_string} which is character version of the \code{family} argument
% (e.g.,~in the default case it will be \code{"gaussian(identity)"}).
% In constructions like those of \cite{parzer2024glms},
% certain data-dependent quantities--such as ridge coefficients--are estimated once at the start and reused across all projections. To support this, the \code{update_fun()} can be used to attach data information as attributes to the \class{randomprojection} object, which are then accessed by \code{generate_fun()}. If provided, \code{update_fun()} should accept \code{...} as its argument; the \code{spar()} function will then internally pass all its own arguments to it. The function must return a \class{randomprojection} object.
% %
% \item In the case where a list of predefined \code{RPMs} is provided in \code{spar()},
% for the data driven random projections we allow the user to
% specify whether some parts of the given \code{RPMs} should be updated with
% the provided data. This is possible through another optional function
% \code{update_rpm_w_data()}.
% This is particularly relevant for the cross-validation procedure,
% which employs the random projection matrices
% generated by calling the \code{spar()} function on the whole data set
% before starting the cross-validation exercise. For example, in our
% implementation of the data-driven \code{rp_cw(data = TRUE)},
% in each fold, we only update the list of \code{RPMs} by adjusting the
% diagonal elements to the vector of screening coefficients computed on
% the training data for the current fold, but do not modify the
% random elements of the projection matrices in each fold, to reduce the
% computational burden.
% Defaults  to \code{NULL}. If not provided, the values of the provided
% \code{RPM}s do not change.
% \end{itemize}
%
% For illustration, consider the built-in \code{rp_gaussian()} function.

% We turn to looking at the structure of the object:
% <<>>=
% unclass(obj)
% @
% The \code{generate_fun()} function returns a matrix with
% \code{m} rows and \code{length(included_vector)} columns.
% Note that \code{included_vector} gives the indices of the
% variables which have been selected by the screening
% procedure. In this case, the random projection does
% not use any data information and we are only interested in the
% length of this vector.

% The function \code{update_fun()} only converts the \class{family} object to a
% string and adds it as an attribute. Function \code{update_rpm_w_data()} is
% \code{NULL} as this random projection is data-agnostic.

A constructor function \code{constructor\_randomprojection()} is provided,
as will be shown in Section~\ref{sec-ext-rp}.
%
\subsection{Marginal models}\label{sec:soft:sparmodel}
%
The \pkg{spareg} package uses the \class{sparmodel} class to define marginal
models for each ensemble element. These models assume a linear predictor
(a linear combination of projected variables).
Functions \code{spar_*()} create these objects and
have arguments \code{...} (to be saved as attributes) and
\code{control} (list used
in the main function for building the model).

The two functions implemented are
\begin{itemize}
\item \code{spar_glm()} fits unregularized GLMs using \code{stats::glm()} (default for Gaussian family with identity link).
\item \code{spar_glmnet()}, which estimates as marginal models
regularized GLMs using function \code{glmnet::glmnet()}
(default for all other families using $\alpha=0$
with the minimal penalty considered in \pkg{glmnet}\footnote{
  In package \pkg{glmnet} $\lambda_\text{min}=\lambda_\text{max}/100$ where
  $\lambda_\text{max}$ is the smallest value that shrinks all coefficients
  to zero and is calculated as the maximum absolute inner product of the
  standardized predictors and the scores evaluated at the null model, divided
  by the number of observations times $\alpha$. For ridge regression
  $\alpha=0.001$ is used computational purposes.})
\end{itemize}
By default, \code{spar_glm()} is used for Gaussian family with identity link,
while \code{spar_glmnet()} is used for all other families.

An object of class `\code{sparmodel}' is a list with elements:
\begin{itemize}
\item an optional character \code{name} for printing;
%
\item  \code{model_fun()} -- a function used to generate the coefficients
and intercept value for each model in the ensemble.
%
\item \code{update_fun()} -- optional function to modify the \class{sparmodel}
object at the start of the SPAR algorithm (e.g.,~adjusting the \class{family}
object for \pkg{glmnet} compatibility).
\end{itemize}
%
An example for implementing a new marginal model class using the constructor
function \code{constructor\_sparmodel()} is presented in
Section~\ref{sec-ext-model}.
%
\subsection{Methods}
%
Methods \fct{print}, \fct{plot}, \fct{coef}, \fct{predict} are available for both `\code{spar}'
and `\code{spar.cv}' classes.
%
\subsubsection[print]{\fct{print}}
The \fct{print} method returns information on $\nu_\text{best}$
$M_\text{best}$, the number of active predictors
(i.e.,~predictors which have at least a non-zero coefficient across the marginal models)
and a summary of the non-zero non-standardized coefficients. For `\fct{spar.cv}'
it shows  information for the $(\nu, M)$
combination chosen by the best and the 1se rule.
<<first_example>>=
set.seed(12)
spar_res <- spar(example_data$x, example_data$y,
  xval = example_data$xtest, yval = example_data$ytest,
  nummods = c(5, 10, 15, 20, 25, 30))
spar_res
@
%
<<first_example_cv, echo = FALSE>>=
set.seed(12)
spar_cv <- spar.cv(example_data$x, example_data$y, nfolds = 3,
  nummods = c(5, 10, 15, 20, 25, 30))
@
%
\subsubsection[coef]{\fct{coef}}
Method \fct{coef} extracts coefficients  with arguments:
\begin{itemize}
\item \code{nummod}, \code{nu}  -- the number of marginal models and threshold
level to use in the calculation of the coefficients. Defaults to the value with
minimal validation error.
%
\item \code{aggregate} --  aggregation method for coefficients  across the
\code{nummod} ensemble models. Options: \code{"mean"}
(default),  \code{"median"} or \code{"none"}(skips the aggregation and returns
the matrix of raw coefficients for each  marginal model -- useful
for understanding the variability of the coefficients across the ensemble models).
\item For \class{spar.cv}, this method is only available for
\code{fast_fit = "fix_rpm_and_inds"}. In this case, \code{opt_par} chooses
between \code{"best"} or \code{"1se"}.   Otherwise, coefficients cannot be
generated without refitting on whole data with \code{"best"} or \code{"1se"}
parameters by calling \fct{spar} with the desired $(M, \nu)$ combination.
\end{itemize}
%
Method \fct{coef} returns an object of class \class{coefspar}, for which
user-friendly \fct{print} and \fct{summary} methods as well as extractor functions
\fct{get\_intercept} and \fct{get\_coef}
are provided.
%
<<eval=FALSE>>=
coef(spar_res)
@
<<echo=FALSE>>=
print(coef(spar_res), show = 5L)
@
<<>>=
get_intercept(coef(spar_res, nummod = 5, aggregate = "none"))
@
%
\subsubsection[predict]{\fct{predict}}
Functionality for computing predictions is provided through the method
\fct{predict} with arguments:
\begin{itemize}
\item \code{xnew} --	new predictor matrix,
%
\item \code{type} --	the type of predictions --  on
\code{"response"} level (default) or on \code{"link"} level.
%
\item  \code{avg_type} -- stage of aggregation:  at level of regression
coefficients (i.e.,~on the \code{link} level) or at the fitted values level
(i.e.,~\code{response} level).
% \begin{itemize}
%     \item \code{"link"} (default): combines models via an aggregation of the regression coefficients (i.e.,~on the \emph{link level}).
%     \item \code{"response"}: combines models via the fitted values by taking computing the predictions of each model in the ensemble and then aggregating on the \emph{response level}.
% \end{itemize}
%
\item \code{nummod}, \code{nu} --	number of models and threshold level used
to compute the averaged coefficients; defaults to optimal values.
%
\item \code{aggregate} -- aggregation method (\code{"mean"} or \code{"median"}).
Defaults to mean aggregation.
%
\item For \class{spar.cv}, this method is only available for
\code{fast_fit = "fix_rpm_and_inds"}. In this case, \code{opt_par} chooses
between \code{"best"} or \code{"1se"}.   Otherwise, predictions cannot be
generated without refitting on whole data with \code{"best"} or \code{"1se"}
parameters. To generate predictions, \fct{spar} can be called to refit the
ensembles with the desired $(M, \nu)$ combination.
\end{itemize}
%
% <<eval=FALSE>>=
% pred <- predict(spar_res, xnew = example_data$xtest)
% pred_cv <- predict(spar_cv, xnew = example_data$xtest, opt_par = "1se")
% @
%
\subsubsection[plot]{\fct{plot}}
%
Plotting functionality is provided through the \fct{plot} method with arguments:
\begin{itemize}
\item \code{plot\_type} -- one of:
\begin{itemize}
\item \code{"val_measure"} plots the (cross-)validation \code{measure} for
either a grid of \code{nu} values for a fixed number of models \code{nummod} or
viceversa.
%
\item \code{"val_numactive"} plots the number of active variables for either a grid
of \code{nu} values for a fixed number of models \code{nummod} or viceversa.
%
\item \code{"res_vs_fitted"} produces a residuals-vs-fitted plot. The residuals
are computed as $r_i=y_i- \widehat y_i$, where $\widehat y_i$ is the prediction computed
on response level (available for \class{spar} objects and
\class{spar.cv} object with \code{fast_fit = "fix_rpm_and_inds"}).
%
\item \code{"coefs"}  produces a plot of the value of the standardized
coefficients for each predictor in each marginal model (before thresholding).
For each predictor, the coefficient values are sorted from largest to
smallest across the marginal models and their value is represented in the plot
using a color scale  (available for \class{spar} objects and
\class{spar.cv} object with \code{fast_fit = "fix_rpm_and_inds"}).
\end{itemize}
\item \code{plot_along} -- for
\code{plot_type = "val_measure"} or
\code{"val_numactive"} indicates whether to vary \code{nu} or \code{nummod} on the $x$~axis;.
%
\item \code{nummod} -- fixed value for number of models when
\code{plot_along = "nu"} for \code{plot_type = "val_measure"} or
\code{"val_numactive"};  if \code{plot_type = "res_vs_fitted"}, it is used in \code{predict()}.
%
\item \code{nu} -- fixed value for  $\nu$ when \code{plot_along = "nummod"}
for \code{plot_type = "val_measure"} or \code{"val_numactive"}; if
\code{plot_type = "res_vs_fitted"}, it is used in  \code{predict()}.
\item \code{xfit} and \code{yfit} -- predictor matrix and response vector for \code{plot_type = "res_vs_fitted"}, as well as \code{prange}, \code{coef_order} --
optional customization parameters for \code{plot_type = "coefs"}.
\end{itemize}
%
The \fct{plot} methods return objects of class \class{ggplot} \citep{ggplotR}.
The four types of plots described above for a \class{spar} object are
shown in Figure~\ref{fig:plot_example}.
%
% <<eval=FALSE>>=
% plot(spar_res)
% plot(spar_res, plot_type = "val_numactive")
% plot(spar_res,  plot_type = "coefs")
% plot(spar_res, plot_type = "res_vs_fitted", xfit = example_data$xtest,
%   yfit = example_data$ytest)
% @
%
\begin{figure}[t!]
\centering
\setkeys{Gin}{width=\textwidth}
<<plotmethod, echo=FALSE, fig=TRUE, width=14, height=4.5,prefix.string=spareg>>=
library(ggplot2)
p1 <- plot(spar_res)+
  theme(axis.text.x = element_text(angle = 45,vjust = 0.5))
p2 <- plot(spar_res, plot_type = "val_numactive") +
  theme(axis.text.x = element_text(angle = 45,vjust = 0.5))
p4 <- plot(spar_res, plot_type = "res_vs_fitted", xfit = example_data$xtest,
  yfit = example_data$ytest)
p3 <- plot(spar_res,  plot_type = "coefs")
p <- ggpubr::ggarrange(p1, p2, p3, p4,
  ncol = 4, nrow = 1)
p
@
\caption{\fct{plot} methods for \class{spar} object. The red dots in the first
two figures show the best $\nu$ chosen on the validation set for the optimal number
of models $M=5$.
\label{fig:plot_example}}
\end{figure}
%
\subsection{Parallelization}
%
The package supports parallelization in the estimation of the
ensemble models via package \pkg{foreach} \citep{pkg:foreach}.
This is possible by setting the parameter \code{parallel = TRUE} in \code{spar()} or
\code{spar.cv()},
assuming that a parallel backend for \pkg{foreach} is registered
using the \code{registerDoParallel()}
function of package \pkg{doParallel} \citep{pkg:doParallel}.
To ensure reproducibility the \pkg{doRNG} \citep{pkg:doRNG}
can be used.
%
Our experiments suggest that parallelization
pays off especially for data sets with a larger number of observations~$n$.
A minimal example with a correlation-based
probabilistic screening and a Gaussian random projection matrix
is provided below:
<<parallel_example,message=FALSE,eval=FALSE>>=
ex4 <- simulate_spareg_data(n = 1000, p = 2000, ntest = 1000, seed = 123)
library(doParallel)
library(doRNG)
cl <- makeCluster(2, type = "PSOCK")
registerDoParallel(cl)
registerDoRNG(seed = 123)
spar_res_par <- spar(ex4$x, ex4$y, screencoef = screen_cor(),
  rp = rp_gaussian(), nummods = 50, parallel = TRUE)
stopCluster(cl)
@
%
\section{Extensibility} \label{sec-extensibility}

\subsection{Screening coefficients} \label{sec-extensscrcoef}
%
We demonstrate how screening coefficients implemented in package
\pkg{VariableScreening} can easily be incorporated in the framework of
\pkg{spareg}.

We start by defining the function for generating the screening coefficients
using function \code{screenIID()} in \pkg{VariableScreening}.
<<>>=
generate_scr_sirs <- function(y, x, object) {
  res_screen <- do.call(function(...)
    VariableScreening::screenIID(x, y, ...), object$control)
  res_screen$measurement
}
@
%
Note that \code{screenIID()} also takes \code{method} as an argument.
To allow for flexibility, we do not
fix the screening method in \code{generate_scr_sirs()} but rather allow the
user to pass a method through the \code{control} argument in the
\code{screen_*()} function. This function is created using the helper
\code{constructor_screencoef()}:
<<sirs_chunk>>=
screen_sirs <- constructor_screencoef(generate_fun = generate_scr_sirs,
  name = "screen_sirs")
@
%
We now call \code{spar()} with the newly created screening procedure.
We consider the method SIRS of \cite{zhu2011model}, which ranks the predictors
by their correlation with the rank-ordered response and we do not perform
probabilistic variable screening but employ the top $2n$ variables in
each marginal model.
The employed random projection is \code{rp_sparse()} where we
set $\psi=1/\sqrt{p}$, as proposed by \cite{LiHastie2006VerySparseRP}.
%
<<screen_sirs>>=
set.seed(123)
spar_example <- spar(example_data$x, example_data$y,
  screencoef = screen_sirs(type = "fixed", control = list(method = "SIRS")),
  rp = rp_sparse(psi = 1/sqrt(ncol(example_data$x))), measure = "mse")
@
%
\subsection{Random projections}\label{sec-ext-rp}
%
To extend the package, we incorporate the random projection method proposed by  \cite{cannings2017random},
which uses the Haar measure to generate orthogonal matrices.
This method involves drawing entries from a standard normal distribution and applying QR decomposition.
%
<<>>=
simulate_haar <- function(m, p) {
  R0 <- matrix(1/sqrt(p) * rnorm(p * m), nrow = p, ncol = m)
  t(qr.Q(qr(R0), complete = FALSE))
}
@
%
\cite{cannings2017random} propose selecting ``good'' random projections
to optimize out-of-sample prediction. Specifically, for each of the models in
the ensemble, $B_2$ Haar random projections are generated. The projection with
the lowest error on a test set (a random subset of proportion $\xi$ of the
data) is selected for projecting the variables. While the $B_2$ projections are
data-agnostic, the entire dataset is required to identify the best projection.

In the implementation, we
fit a GLM as a marginal model on the training data.
The performance is evaluated using deviance on the test set. Default values can be specified in \code{update_fun()}:
% related to the family. Given that we will rely again on
% \code{glmnet::glmnet()} in each iteration~$b$,
% the family object is modified to a
% string containing the name of the family for families Gaussian, binomial and
% Poisson with canonical link (this will lead to using faster specialized
% algorithms). This modified family is
% added to the list of controls as \code{fit\_family}.
<<update_cannings>>=
update_rp_cannings <- function(...) {
  args <- rlang::list2(...)
  if (is.null(args$rp$control$alpha)) args$rp$control$alpha <- 0
  if (is.null(args$rp$control$B2))  args$rp$control$B2 <- 50
  if (is.null(args$rp$control$xi)) args$rp$control$xi <- 0.25
  if (is.null(args$rp$control$family)) args$rp$control$family <-
      args$family
  args$rp
}
@
%
Next we specify the function for generating the random projection.
<<generate_cannings>>=
generate_cannings <- function(rp, m, included_vector, x, y) {
  xs <- x[, included_vector]; n <- nrow(x);  p <- ncol(xs)
  id_test <- sample(n, size = n * rp$control$xi)
  xtrain <- xs[-id_test, ];  xtest <- xs[id_test,]
  ytrain <- y[-id_test];  ytest <- y[id_test]
  control_glm <-
    rp$control[names(rp$control) %in% names(formals(stats::glm.fit))]
  best_val <- Inf
  for (b in seq_len(rp$control$B2)) {
    RM <- simulate_haar(m, p)
    xrp <- tcrossprod(xtrain, RM)
    mod <- do.call(function(...)
      glm.fit(x = cbind(1, xrp), y = ytrain, ...), control_glm)
    eta_test <- (cbind(1, tcrossprod(xtest, RM)) %*% coef(mod))
    pred <- rp$control$family$linkinv(eta_test)
    out_dev <- sum(rp$control$family$dev.resid(ytest, pred, wt = 1))
    if (out_dev < best_val) {
      best_val <- out_dev; best_RM <- RM
    }
  }
  return(best_RM)
}
@
Putting it all together, we get:
<<define_rp_cannings>>=
rp_cannings <- constructor_randomprojection(
  name = "RP Cannings and Samworth 2017",
  generate_fun = generate_cannings, update_fun = update_rp_cannings)
@
%
We can now estimate SPAR for a binomial model, after simulating
data from a linear
model and then transforming the response to a binary scale:
<<data_cannings>>=
set.seed(1234)
ex2 <- simulate_spareg_data(n = 200, p = 500, ntest = 100)
ys <- (ex2$y > 0) + 0; ysval <- (ex2$ytest > 0) + 0
@
We use only one value for the number of models $M=50$ \citep[which is in line
to recommendations in][]{cannings2017random}, no screening and no thresholding.
We set the maximum dimension for the random projection to $m_u = 20$
to avoid generating large matrices and for stability in the marginal binomial
GLMs.
<<rp_cannings_1,eval=TRUE>>=
set.seed(1234)
spar_cs <- spar(x = ex2$x, y = ys, family = binomial(),
  rp = rp_cannings(msup = 20), model = spar_glm(),
  nus = 0, nummods = 50, xval = ex2$xtest,  yval = ysval)
@
We can compare with results obtained by using the data-agnostic
sparse embedding matrix \code{rp_cw(data = FALSE)}:
<<rp_cannings_2, eval=TRUE>>=
set.seed(1234)
spar_cw <- spar(x = ex2$x, y = ys, family = binomial(),
  rp = rp_cw(data = FALSE, msup = 20), model = spar_glm(),
  nus = 0, nummods = 50, xval = ex2$xtest, yval = ysval)
@
We can compare the two approaches by directly looking at the validation measure
achieved by the two ensembles on the provided validation set.
The extractor function \code{get_measure()} can be used to extract the
following information from the \class{spar} object:
<<compare>>=
rbind("cannings" = get_measure(spar_cs), "cw" = get_measure(spar_cw))
@
%
\subsection{Marginal models}\label{sec-ext-model}
%
Finally, we extend the package by implementing a robust GLM as a marginal model
using the package \pkg{robustbase} \citep{pkg:robustbase}.

We define a \code{model_fun()} that uses \code{robustbase::lmrob()} for Gaussian
models with identity link and \code{robustbase::glmrob()}
for other family-link combinations.
The function has arguments are \code{y} (response vector),
\code{z} (matrix of reduced predictors)
and a `\code{sparmodel}' \code{object},
and outputs a list with two elements: \code{gammas} (vector of coefficients)
and the \code{intercept} value.
<<>>=
model_glmrob <- function(y, z, object) {
  requireNamespace("robustbase")
  fam <- object$control$family
  f <- ifelse(fam$family == "gaussian" & fam$link == "identity",
    robustbase::lmrob, robustbase::glmrob)
  glmrob_res <- do.call(function(...)
    f(y ~ as.matrix(z), ...), object$control)
  cfs <- coef(glmrob_res)
  list(gammas = cfs[-1], intercept = cfs[1])
}
@
%
Function \code{spar_glmrob()} that builds the `\code{sparmodel}'
object can be created by:
%
<<spar_glmrob>>=
spar_glmrob <- constructor_sparmodel(model_fun = model_glmrob)
@
%
We demonstrate this on simulated Poisson data with 25\% contaminated predictors:
%
<<generate_example_data3>>=
set.seed(123)
example_data3 <- simulate_spareg_data(n = 100, p = 1000,
  ntest = 100, snr = 10, beta_vals = c(-1, 1)/10)
ypois <- round(exp(example_data3$y)) + 1; x <- example_data3$x;
np <- ncol(x) * nrow(x)
id_outliers_x <- sample(seq_len(np),  0.25 * np)
x[id_outliers_x] <- x[id_outliers_x] + 50
@
%
We then compare SPAR with robust GLM marginal models
to standard GLMs,
using  no screening, \code{rp_gaussian(msup = 25)} --
to improve stability we override the default for \code{msup} of
$n/2=\Sexpr{round(nrow(x)/2)}$ --
and MAE as the selection criterion:
<<run_glmrob_glm>>=
set.seed(1234)
spar_rob_res <- spar(x, ypois, family = poisson(),
  model = spar_glmrob(), rp = rp_gaussian(msup = 25), measure = "mae")
set.seed(1234)
spar_res <- spar(x, ypois, family = poisson(),
  model = spar_glm(), rp = rp_gaussian(msup = 25), measure = "mae")
@
%
The \code{get_model()} function extracts the optimal ensemble
from a \class{spar}  or \class{spar.cv} object.
It selects either the best model (\code{opt_par = "best"}, default)
or, for \class{spar.cv} objects,
the model based on the 1se rule (\code{opt_par = "1se"}).
<<compare_glmrob_glm>>=
best_rob <- get_model(spar_rob_res, opt_par = "best")
best_glm <- get_model(spar_res, opt_par = "best")
@
The validation measure for each of these models is given below.
The robust version achieves lower MAE, with all predictors retained in the
ensemble (each has at least one non-zero coefficient across models).
<<compare_glmrob_glm>>=
rbind("robglm" = get_measure(best_rob), "glm" = get_measure(best_glm))
@
%
\ifPositive

\section{Illustrations} \label{sec-illustrations}
%
\subsection{Face image data}
%
We illustrate \pkg{spareg} on the Isomap data set
containing $n = 698$
grayscale face images of size $p = 64 \times 64 = 4096$ together
with horizontal looking direction angles as response. The data set is publicly
available for download at
\url{https://web.archive.org/web/20150922051706/http://isomap.stanford.edu/face_data.mat.Z}.
<<urls, echo=FALSE>>=
if (is_paper) {
  url1 <- "https://web.archive.org/web/20150922051706/"
  url2 <- "http://isomap.stanford.edu/face_data.mat.Z"
  ## On Ubuntu or MacOS use code below
  ## On Windows, download locally and unzip
  if (!file.exists(file.path(tmpdir, "face_data.mat"))) {
    # system('uncompress face_data.mat.Z')
    library("R.matlab")
    download.file(paste0(url1, url2),
                  file.path(tmpdir, "face_data.mat.Z"))
    system(sprintf('uncompress %s', file.path(tmpdir, "face_data.mat.Z")))
  }
}
@
<<unzip_file,echo=FALSE,message=FALSE>>=
if (is_paper) {
  library("R.matlab")
  facedata <- readMat(file.path(tmpdir,"face_data.mat"))
  x <- t(facedata$images)
  y <- facedata$poses[1,]
  x[, apply(x, 2, sd) < 0.01] <- 0
}
@
%
After downloading and uncompressing the \code{.mat} file, we import it into \proglang{R}
using package \pkg{R.matlab} \citep{pkg:rmatlab}. Moreover, the data set
contains (nearly) constant columns. Functions \code{spar()} and \code{spar.cv()}
ignore constant columns from the estimation,
but we also set columns with a low standard deviation to zero. One image in
the data set is presented in the left panel of Figure~\ref{fig:faces_predictions}.
<<read_matlab,eval=FALSE>>=
library("R.matlab")
facedata <- readMat("face_data.mat")
x <- t(facedata$images); y <- facedata$poses[1, ]
x[, apply(x, 2, sd) < 0.01] <- 0
@
%
%
We split the data into 75\% training vs 25\%
test sample:
<<std_x,echo=FALSE>>=
if (is_paper) {
  set.seed(123)
  ntot <- length(y); ntest <- ntot * 0.25
  testind <- sample(ntot, ntest, replace = FALSE)
  xtrain <- as.matrix(x[-testind, ]); ytrain <- y[-testind]
  xtest <- as.matrix(x[testind, ]); ytest <- y[testind]
}
@
<<eval=FALSE>>=
set.seed(123)
testind <- sample(length(y), length(y) * 0.25, replace = FALSE)
xtrain <- as.matrix(x[-testind, ]); ytrain <- y[-testind]
xtest <- as.matrix(x[testind, ]); ytest <- y[testind]
@
We estimate the SPAR algorithm with cross-validation as in
\cite{parzer2024sparse} (with \code{lambda.min.ratio = 0.001}
to ensure convergence of the algorithm when computing the ridge screening
coefficients) and use a grid of $M=5,10,20,50$ models.
<<echo=FALSE>>=
if (is_paper) {
  file <- file.path(tmpdir, "faces_res.rda")
  if (!file.exists(file)) {
    set.seed(123)
    control_glmnet <- list(lambda.min.ratio = 0.001)
    library("spareg")
    Sys.time()
    spar_faces <- spar.cv(
      xtrain, ytrain,
      model = spar_glm(),
      screencoef = screen_glmnet(control = control_glmnet,
                                 reuse_in_rp = TRUE),
      rp = rp_cw(data = TRUE),
      nummods = c(5, 10, 20, 50),
      measure = "mse")
    Sys.time()
    save(spar_faces, file = file)
  } else {
    load(file)
  }
}
@
<<eval=FALSE>>=
set.seed(123)
control_glmnet <- list(lambda.min.ratio = 0.001)
spar_faces <- spar.cv(xtrain, ytrain,
  model = spar_glm(), rp = rp_cw(data = TRUE),
  screencoef = screen_glmnet(control = control_glmnet, reuse_in_rp = TRUE),
  nummods = c(5, 10, 20, 50), measure = "mse")
@
%\subsubsection{Results}
The \fct{print} method returns the following information about the fitted
\class{spar.cv} object. The combination $(M=20, \nu=0)$
 delivers the lowest MSE while the 1se rule chooses $(M=5, \nu=0.00424)$, with
 small differences in performance.
%
<<eval=FALSE>>=
spar_faces
@
<<echo=FALSE>>=
if (is_paper) spar_faces
@
%
The \code{plot} method for `\code{spar.cv}' objects displays by default the
measure employed in the cross-validation (in this case MSE)
for a grid of thresholding values $\nu$, where the number of models is fixed to
the value found to perform best in cross-validation
exercise
(Figure~\ref{fig:facesplot_valmeasure} top panel). Results for $M=5$, which
is the value chosen by the 1se rule, can be visualized by setting
\code{nummod = 5} (Figure~\ref{fig:facesplot_valmeasure} bottom panel).
%
<<plotfacesmeasure, eval=FALSE>>=
plot(spar_faces)
plot(spar_faces, nummod = 5)
@
\begin{figure}[t!]
\centering
\setkeys{Gin}{width=0.95\textwidth}
<<plotfacesmeasurereal_best, echo=FALSE, fig=TRUE, width=8, height=3,prefix.string=spareg>>=
if (is_paper) {
  ## best
  p <- plot(spar_faces, digits = 3L) +
    theme(axis.text.x = element_text(angle = 45, vjust = 1,
                                     hjust = 1))
  p
}
@
<<plotfacesmeasurereal_1se, echo=FALSE, fig=TRUE, width=8, height=3,prefix.string=spareg>>=
if (is_paper) {
  ## 1se
  p2 <- plot(spar_faces, nummod=5, digits = 3L) +
    theme(axis.text.x = element_text(angle = 45, vjust = 1,
                                     hjust = 1))
  p2
}
@
\caption{Mean squared error vs. a grid of threshold values $\nu$ for fixed
number of models -- chosen by best (top) and 1SE rule (bottom) for the
\emph{Isomap faces} data set.
Red points correspond to the best (minimal MSE) and sparsest ensemble model
within 1SE of the best, respectively. Confidence bands represent one standard
deviation in the MSE across folds. \label{fig:facesplot_valmeasure}}
\end{figure}
%
The coefficients of the different variables (in this example pixels) obtained by
averaging over the coefficients the marginal models can be extracted by \fct{coef}. For the ensemble chosen by the 1SE rule we have:
%
<<facecoef1se, eval=FALSE>>=
face_coef_1se <- coef(spar_faces, opt_par = "1se")
summary(face_coef_1se)
@
<<facecoef1se,echo=FALSE>>=
if (is_paper) {
  face_coef_1se <- coef(spar_faces, opt_par = "1se")
  summary(face_coef_1se)
}
@
%
With this vector of coefficients we visualize
the effect of each pixel $\hat\beta^\text{1se}_j x^\text{new}_{i,j}$ in
predicting the face orientation in a given image in the right panel of
Figure~\ref{fig:faces_predictions}, where face contours and hairlines are most
predictive for orientation.
%

Finally we compare the performance of the estimated version of the SPAR algorithm
with another version proposed in \cite{Dunson2020TargRandProj}, where the sparse
random projection in \cite{ACHLIOPTAS2003JL} is combined with
probabilistic marginal correlation screening.
We also use the values of $m_l=2\log p$ and $m_u=3/4 n$ as recommended in \cite{Dunson2020TargRandProj} to define the lower and upper bounds for the random projection dimension.
%
<<tarp_comparison_true,eval=FALSE>>=
set.seed(123)
m_l <- 2 * log(ncol(xtrain)); m_u <- 3/4 * nrow(xtrain)
tarp_faces <-  spar.cv(xtrain, ytrain, screencoef = screen_cor(),
  rp = rp_sparse(psi = 1/3, mslow = m_l, msup = m_u), model = spar_glm(),
  nummods = c(5, 10, 20, 50), measure = "mse")
@
<<tarp_comparison_true,echo=FALSE>>=
if(is_paper) {
  set.seed(123)
  m_l <- 2 * log(ncol(xtrain)); m_u <- 3/4 * nrow(xtrain)
  tarp_faces <-  spar.cv(xtrain, ytrain,
                         model = spar_glm(),
                         screencoef = screen_cor(),
                         rp = rp_sparse(psi = 1/3, mslow = m_l, msup = m_u),
                         nummods = c(5, 10, 20, 50), measure = "mse")
  ynew_tarp <- predict(tarp_faces, xnew = xtest, opt_par = "1se")
}
@
%
The predictive performance on the \emph{test data} can be assessed by employing
the \code{predict()} method on the `\code{spar.cv}' object.
We will use the sparser solution chosen by the \code{opt_par = "1se"} rule and
the default mean aggregation for the predictions:
%
<<test_tarp_comparison_false,eval=FALSE>>=
ynew_spar <- predict(spar_faces, opt_par = "1se", xnew = xtest)
ynew_tarp <- predict(tarp_faces, opt_par = "1se", xnew = xtest)
c("SPAR" = mean((ytest - ynew_spar)^2),
  "TARP" = mean((ytest - ynew_tarp)^2))
@
<<test_tarp_comparison_true,echo=FALSE>>=
if(is_paper) {
ynew_spar <- predict(spar_faces, opt_par = "1se", xnew = xtest)
ynew_tarp <- predict(tarp_faces, opt_par = "1se", xnew = xtest)
  c("SPAR" = mean((ytest - ynew_spar)^2),
    "TARP" = mean((ytest - ynew_tarp)^2))}
@
%
\begin{figure}[t!]
\centering
\setkeys{Gin}{width=0.45\textwidth}
<<plotfacesobs179, fig=TRUE,echo=FALSE,width=4.5,height=4.5,prefix.string=spareg>>=
if (is_paper) {
  i <- 179
  p <- ggplot(data.frame(X = rep(1:64,each=64),
                         Y = rep(64:1,64),
                         Z = facedata$images[,i]),
              aes(X, Y, fill = Z)) +
    geom_tile() +
    theme_void() +
    ggtitle(paste0("y = ", round(facedata$poses[1, i],1))) +
    theme(legend.position = "none",
          plot.title = element_text(hjust = 0.5))
  p
}
@
<<plotobsvspred179,fig=TRUE,echo=FALSE,width=4.5,height=4.5,prefix.string=spareg>>=
if (is_paper) {
  id <- 3
  p4 <- ggplot(data.frame(X = rep(1:64, each = 64),
                          Y = rep(64:1, 64),
                          effect = xtest[id,] * face_coef_1se$beta),
               aes(X, Y, fill = effect)) +
    geom_tile() +
    theme_void() +
    scale_fill_gradient2() +
    ggtitle(bquote(hat(y) == .(round(ynew_spar[id]))), ) +
    theme(plot.title = element_text(hjust = 0.5))
  p4
}
@
\caption{Left: Image corresponding to one observation in the
\emph{Isomap faces} data set. Right: The effect of each pixel
$\hat\beta^\text{1se}_j x^\text{new}_{i,j}$ in predicting the face
orientation in left panel. \label{fig:faces_predictions}}
\end{figure}
%
\subsection{Darwin data set}
%
The Darwin data set \citep{CILIA2022darwin} contains a binary response for
Alzheimer's disease (AD) together with extracted features from 25 handwriting
tests (18 features per task) for 89 AD patients and 85 healthy people ($n=174$).
The data set can be downloaded from
\url{https://archive.ics.uci.edu/dataset/732/darwin} as a \code{.zip} file.
The corresponding \code{.csv} file can then be imported into \proglang{R}.
<<readdarwinreal, echo=FALSE,message=FALSE>>=
if (is_paper) {
  # if (file.exists(file.path(tmpdir, "data.zip"))) {
  #  unzip(file.path(tmpdir, "data.zip")exdir = )
  #} else {
  if (!file.exists(file.path(tmpdir, "darwin.zip"))) {
    download.file("https://archive.ics.uci.edu/static/public/732/darwin.zip",
                  file.path(tmpdir, "darwin.zip"))
    unzip(zipfile = file.path(tmpdir, "darwin.zip"),
          files = "data.csv",
          exdir = tmpdir)
  }
  darwin_tmp <- read.csv(file.path(tmpdir, "data.csv"),
                         stringsAsFactors = TRUE)
}
@
<<readdarwinzip,eval=FALSE>>=
darwin_tmp <- read.csv("data.csv", stringsAsFactors = TRUE)
@
Before proceeding with the analysis, the data is screened for
multivariate outliers using the DDC algorithm in package
\pkg{cellWise} \citep{rcellwise}.
<<darwinimpute,eval=FALSE>>=
darwin_orig <- list(y = as.numeric(darwin_tmp$class) - 1,
  x = darwin_tmp[, !(colnames(darwin_tmp) %in% c("ID", "class"))])
tmp <- cellWise::DDC(as.matrix(darwin_orig$x),
  list(returnBigXimp = TRUE, tolProb = 0.999, silent = TRUE))
darwin <- list(x = tmp$Ximp, y = darwin_orig$y)
@
<<echo=FALSE,message=FALSE>>=
if (is_paper) {
  darwin_orig <- list(
    x = darwin_tmp[, !(colnames(darwin_tmp) %in% c("ID", "class"))],
    y = as.numeric(darwin_tmp$class) - 1)
  tmp <- suppressMessages(cellWise::DDC(
    as.matrix(darwin_orig$x),
    list(returnBigXimp = TRUE, tolProb = 0.999, silent = TRUE)))
  darwin <- list(x = tmp$Ximp, y = darwin_orig$y)
}
@
% The structure of the data is:
% <<darwinstr,eval=FALSE>>=
% str(darwin)
% @
% <<echo=FALSE,message=FALSE>>=
% if (is_paper) str(darwin)
% @
%
We estimate the SPAR algorithm with the screening and random projection
introduced in \cite{parzer2024glms} for binomial family and logit link,
using $1-$area under the ROC curve as the cross-validation measure:
<<spardarwin, eval=FALSE>>=
set.seed(1234)
spar_darwin <- spar.cv(darwin$x, darwin$y, family = binomial(logit),
  screencoef = screen_glmnet(reuse_in_rp = TRUE), nummods = c(10, 20, 30, 50),
  measure = "1-auc")
spar_darwin
@
<<spardarwinreal, echo=FALSE>>=
if (is_paper) {
  file <- file.path(tmpdir, "darwin_res.rda")
  if (!file.exists(file)) {
    set.seed(1234)
    spar_darwin <- spareg::spar.cv(darwin$x, darwin$y,
                                   family = binomial(logit),
                                   screencoef = screen_glmnet(reuse_in_rp = TRUE),
                                   nummods = c(10, 20, 30, 50),
                                   measure = "1-auc")
    save(spar_darwin, file = file)
  } else {
    load(file = file)
  }
  spar_darwin
}
@
No thresholding achieves the best measure and translates to almost all variables
being active (some variables can be inactive at $\nu=0$
as they may never be screened).
The 1se rule  achieves that a much sparser model with a cross-validation measure
of around 0.075. The average number of active variables for the grid of thresholds
$\nu$ and $M = 20$ models is shown in Figure~\ref{fig:darwin_activevars}.
<<plotdarwinact, eval=FALSE>>=
plot(spar_darwin, plot_type = "val_numactive")
@
%
\begin{figure}[t!]
\centering
\setkeys{Gin}{width=0.8\textwidth}
<<plotdarwinactnumactive,fig=TRUE,echo=FALSE,width=8,height=3,prefix.string=spareg>>=
if (is_paper) {
  p <- plot(spar_darwin, plot_type = "val_numactive", digits=3L)
  p
}
@
\caption{Average number of active variables vs. threshold values
$\nu$ and $M=20$ models for the \emph{Darwin} data set. The red points
correspond to the average number of active variables for the ensemble with the
lowest cross-validation measure and to the one chosen by the 1se
rule. \label{fig:darwin_activevars}}
\end{figure}
%

Finally, standardized coefficients of the predictors across the maximum number of
considered marginal models (in this case $M=50$) can be visualized with
option \code{plot_type = "coefs"} in method \code{plot()}.
In this data set, the predictors are ordered by task, where the first 18
covariates represent different features measured for the first task, the
following 18 covariates represent the same features for the second task and
so on.
The predictors naturally group by feature, allowing for a more intuitive
assessment of how each feature (e.g., time spent on a task) relates to the
likelihood of Alzheimer's disease. Reordering the coefficients by feature
-- rather than by task -- can be done by using the reordering argument
\code{coef\_order}. The resulting plot is shown in
Figure~\ref{fig:darwin_coefs}.
We add dashed vertical lines
to separate the features,  and color indicates coefficient sign (blue/red).
Consistent sign patterns across tasks suggest stable feature influences.
The presence of distinct blocks of blue or red lines reveals that the features
measured for different tasks generally exert a consistent --
either positive or negative -- influence on the probability of Alzheimer’s disease.
Line height shows selection frequency, with important features appearing in most models.
%%
\begin{figure}[t!]
\centering
\setkeys{Gin}{width=0.8\textwidth}
<<plotdarwincoef,fig=TRUE,echo=FALSE,width=7,height=4.5,prefix.string=spareg>>=
if (is_paper) {
  ntasks <- 25
  nfeat <- 18
  reorder_ind <- c(outer(
    (seq_len(ntasks) - 1) * nfeat,
    seq_len(nfeat), "+"))
  feat_names <- sapply(colnames(darwin$x)[seq_len(nfeat)],
                       function(name) substr(name, 1, nchar(name) - 1))
  p <- plot(spar_darwin,"coefs",coef_order = reorder_ind) +
    geom_vline(xintercept = 0.5 + seq_len(ntasks - 1) * ntasks,
               alpha = 0.2, linetype = 2) +
    annotate("text",x = (seq_len(nfeat) - 1) * ntasks + 12,
             y = 40,
             label=feat_names, angle = 90,
             size = 3)
  p
}
@
\caption{Coefficient plot for all variables and all considered $M=50$ models
in the SPAR ensemble for the \emph{Darwin} data set. The coefficients are
standardized, before thresholding. \label{fig:darwin_coefs}}
\end{figure}
%%
\fi

\section{Conclusion} \label{sec-conclusion}

Package \pkg{spareg} can be employed for modeling high-dimensional data in a GLM
framework, especially in settings where the number of predictors is much higher
than the number of observations. The package provides an implementation of a
computationally-efficient algorithm for sparse
projected and average regression (SPAR),
which combines variable screening and random projection in an ensemble of
GLMs to reduce dimensionality of the predictors.
Package \pkg{spareg} provides flexible classes for
i)~specifying the
coefficient based on which screening should be performed (both in a classical
fashion, where the predictors with the highest screening coefficient
are selected for subsequent analysis or in a probabilistic fashion, where
variables are sampled for inclusion with probabilities proportional
to their screening coefficient), ii)~generating the random projection
to be employed in each marginal model, iii)~specifying the marginal models to be
used in the ensemble. Screening coefficients based on
marginal correlation between the predictors and the response, marginal
coefficients from a GLM or ridge coefficients are provided in the package.
Moreover, several random projections are implemented:
the Gaussian and sparse matrices which are data-agnostic and satisfy the JL
lemma and the data-driven projection proposed in \cite{parzer2024sparse}
for linear
regression and extended to GLMs in \cite{parzer2024glms}.  This data-driven
approach, where information about the relationship among the responses and the
predictors is incorporated in the random projection by replacing the non-zero
elements of the random projection of \cite{Clarkson2013LowRankApprox} with the
ridge coefficients obtained with a small penalty, has the
advantage of ensuring that the true
regression coefficients can be recovered approximately after the projection.
Methodologically, the SPAR algorithm with ridge screening coefficients and
the data-driven random projection  \citep[as proposed in][]{parzer2024glms}
has been demonstrated to perform effectively in terms of predictive power and
variable ranking in settings with correlated predictors and across different
degrees of sparsity of the coefficient
vector, making it a suitable method for high-dimensional settings with correlated
predictors where the sparsity of the problem is unknown. This is the motivation
behind setting the random projection in \cite{parzer2024glms} as a default
in \pkg{spareg}.

Moreover, the flexibility and adaptability of the \pkg{spareg} package in
dealing with different types of screening, random projection and types of
GLMs, make it an
attractive choice for practitioners and researchers in a
wide variety of data settings.
It encourages exploration
of new methods for variable screening and random projections or the
combination of existing approaches to tailor solutions to specific data
requirements.

\section*{Computational details}

The results in this paper were obtained using \proglang{R}
\Sexpr{paste(R.Version()[6:7], collapse = ".")}.
\proglang{R} itself and all packages used are available from CRAN
at \url{https://CRAN.R-project.org/}.

