\section[Simulation Studies]{Simulation Study} \label{app:simulation-study}
\subsection[Computational efficiency and memory usage of spareg]{Computational efficiency and memory usage of \pkg{spareg}}
%%
To evaluate the computational efficiency and memory usage of the algorithm
implemented in \pkg{spareg}, we conducted a large-scale simulation study for the
linear regression setting comparing its performance against Elastic Net (ElNet)
under varying sample sizes ($n$) and numbers of predictors ($p$). The study
included 100 repetitions for each of 9 simulation settings. For investigating
the effect of increasing $p$ we cover combinations
of $n = 200$ and $p = 500, 1000, 2000, 5000, 10000, 20000$.
For investigating  the effect of increasing $n$ we cover combinations
$p = 2000$ and $n = 100, 200, 400, 800$. For each setting,
synthetic data from a linear regression model were generated using the
\code{simulate_spareg_data()} function with
default parameters: $100$ non-zero coefficients, a signal-to-noise ratio of 10,
and a compound symmetry correlation structure ($\rho = 0.5$).
We evaluated 7 methods:
%%
\begin{itemize}
\item SPAR (default): no screening, data-dependent random projection
  \code{rp\_cw(data = TRUE)},
\item SPAR CV (default): no screening,
  \code{rp_cw(data = TRUE)}, 10-fold cross-validation,
\item SPAR screen: screening with ridge
  coefficients \code{screen_glmnet(reuse_in_rp = TRUE)},
  \code{rp\_cw(data = TRUE)}, where the screening coefficients are used in
  constructing the CW random projection matrix),
\item SPAR screen CV (\code{screen\_glmnet(reuse_in_rp=TRUE)} and
  \code{rp\_cw(data = TRUE)}, where the ridge coefficients are used in
  constructing the CW random projection matrix,  and 10-fold cross-validation),
\item SPAR screen cor: \code{screen\_cor(reuse_in_rp=TRUE)} and \code{rp_cw(data = TRUE)}, where the correlation coefficients are used in the CW random projection matrix,
\item SPAR CW: no screening, data-agnostic \code{rp_cw(data = FALSE)},
\item SPAR Gaussian: no screening, \code{rp_gaussian()},
\item ElNet: Elastic Net with 10-fold CV for $\lambda$ and $\alpha=1/2$,
\item ElNet CValpha: Elastic Net with 10-fold CV for both  $\lambda$ and $\alpha$.
\end{itemize}
%%
Performance was assessed using the metrics:
%%
\begin{itemize}
\item computation time (elapsed time in seconds),
\item memory usage (in megabytes),
\item relative mean squared prediction error (rMSPE, relative to a model containing only
an intercept) on a test set
of $n_\text{test}=200$ observations,
\item to evaluate how well the methods are able to rank the variables, we employ the absolute value of the estimated coefficient vector to compute the partial area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (pAUC), where the number of false positives is limited to $n/2$.
\end{itemize}
%%
The results for increasing $n$ are presented in
Figure~\ref{fig:bench_sim_n} while Figure~\ref{fig:bench_sim_p} shows the results
for increasing $p$.
Increasing $n$ leads to the largest increase in computation time and memory usage for the SPAR CV methods.
 In terms of predictive performance, SPAR and its variants using the data driven
 projections outperform on average ElNet for lower $n$, while
 elastic net is superior when $n$ increases.

When increasing $p$, we observe that SPAR and its variants maintain their computational efficiency and memory usage, while ElNetCV's scales worse.
Performance deteriorates in terms of both rMSPE and pAUC when increasing $p$
but SPAR and its data driven variants projections do remain on average superior
to ElNet in terms of predictive performance and variable ranking.
%
\begin{figure}[t!]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.45\textwidth]{sim_bench/CompTime_n.pdf}
\includegraphics[width=0.45\textwidth]{sim_bench/Memory_n.pdf}
\includegraphics[width=0.45\textwidth]{sim_bench/rMSPE_n.pdf}
\includegraphics[width=0.45\textwidth]{sim_bench/pAUC_n.pdf}
\caption{Impact of increasing $n$ with fixed $p=2000$ for different methods in terms of
computation time in seconds (log10 $x$ and $y$~axis, top left panel), memory usage in megabytes (log10 $x$ and $y$~axis, top right panel),
rMSPE (bottom left panel), pAUC (bottom right panel).
\label{fig:bench_sim_n}}
\end{figure}
%
\begin{figure}[t!]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.45\textwidth]{sim_bench/CompTime_p.pdf}
\includegraphics[width=0.45\textwidth]{sim_bench/Memory_p.pdf}
\includegraphics[width=0.45\textwidth]{sim_bench/rMSPE_p.pdf}
\includegraphics[width=0.45\textwidth]{sim_bench/pAUC_p.pdf}
\caption{Impact of increasing $p$ with fixed $n=200$ for different methods in terms of
computation time in seconds (log10 $x$ and $y$~axis, top left panel), memory usage in megabytes (log10 $x$ and $y$~axis, top right panel),
rMSPE (bottom left panel), pAUC (bottom right panel).
\label{fig:bench_sim_p}}
\end{figure}




\subsection{Comparison of fast fitting options in cross-validation} \label{app:cv-fast-fit}

We perform a simulation study by generating synthetic datasets with 100
training and 100 validation samples, and 1000 predictors,
where the first 25 coefficients are non-zero.
We test two configurations
\code{rp\_gaussian()} + \code{screen\_marglik()}, and
\code{rp\_cw(data=TRUE)} + \code{screen\_glmnet()}.
For each configuration, three fast-fit options are evaluated:
\code{"fix\_rpm\_and\_inds"} (fixing both random projection matrices and screened indices, default),
\code{"fix\_rpm"} (fixing only the random projection matrices but computing the
screening coefficients on each fold)
and
\code{"none"} (full cross-validation),

The performance is measured using mean squared prediction error (MSE) for Gaussian models
and deviance for logistic models, while runtime is recorded for computational
efficiency. The experiment is repeated 50 times. Results are
visualized in Figure~\ref{fig:cvmodes}.
We observe that the performance is
similar across the three fast-fit modes,
and the runtime is also rather similar.
%
